// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): "Every
// potentially-evaluated call to it shall produce a constant expression. If it
// does not, the program is ill-formed." A `constexpr` function's own
// parameter has no known value while the function is being checked, so
// `twice(x)` is not a constant expression and the program is ill-formed --
// even though `outer` is itself `constexpr` and even though the single call
// site passes a literal.
//
// The C++ rule that would otherwise rescue this, immediate escalation, does
// not apply: an immediate-escalating function is only a lambda call operator,
// a non-user-provided defaulted function, or a function instantiated from a
// templated entity defined `constexpr` ([expr.const.imm]/3). A non-templated
// `constexpr` function is none of those, so the immediate-escalating
// expression `twice(x)` has nowhere to escalate to. §9.1(4) reaches the same
// conclusion directly, without needing the escalation machinery.
//
// The positive half of this rule -- the same shape with a literal argument --
// is covered by consteval_call_inside_constexpr_function_folds.scpp.
//
// Rejection independently confirmed with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which
// reports: "error: call to consteval function 'twice' is not a constant
// expression" / "note: function parameter 'x' with unknown value cannot be
// used in a constant expression".
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS the .stderr pin. scpp does reject
// the program, but with "error: internal error: no generated code for
// resolved function 'twice' called here" -- an internal-compiler-error
// report, not a diagnosis of §9.1(4). Do not relax the pin to admit
// `internal error`.
consteval int twice(int x) {
    return x * 2;
}

constexpr int outer(int x) {
    return twice(x);
}

int main() {
    return outer(21);
}
